### Spaces `copernicus/spaces` (11 October 2026)

- Summary: Defines the spaces that the Copernicus network uses. (11 October 2026)
- COP nodes cited: crop, uvmap, xform (11 October 2026)
- Section: Overview (11 October 2026)
- The Copernicus network uses and refers to the following spaces: (11 October 2026)
- * Buffer: The samples of the image, which correspond to the data layout in the memory. (11 October 2026)
- * Pixel: A canonical pixel-sized area. (11 October 2026)
- * Texture: Maps from 0 through 1 across the data window and Buffer space, which stretches the texture. (11 October 2026)
- * Image: Maps from -1 through 1 across the display window, which scales the image to fit to preserve the pixel aspect ratio. (11 October 2026)
- * Local: Maps from -(w/2, h/2) through (w/2, h/2) across the display window, where w and h are the width and height of the display window in world ... (11 October 2026)
- * World: A 3D location in the modelling space. (11 October 2026)
- Instead of always using texture space, Copernicus uses image space. (11 October 2026)
- * You don't need metadata that indicates the value's actual range. (11 October 2026)
- * The values are one-to-one with the SOP version of attribtues. (11 October 2026)
- Section: View images (11 October 2026)
- The 3D viewport imports Copernicus nodes using world space, which by default is the same as image space. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Cropping (11 October 2026)
- When you Crop in image space (set Units to Image), the default crops from -1 to 1. (11 October 2026)
- When you Crop in texture space (set Units to Texture), the node crops the image based on the incoming image's data window. (11 October 2026)
- When you Crop in pixel space (set Units to Pixels), the node crops the image based on the exact pixel values you set in the node. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Visualization (11 October 2026)
- Visualizing refers to when you perform UV mapping in the viewport instead of displaying the real colors of an image. (11 October 2026)
- When you wire a Crop node into a UV Map that has UV Space set to Image, the output displays the remaining section in the color that originally ... (11 October 2026)
- When you wire a Crop node into a UV Map that has UV Space set to Texture, the output displays the remaining section in the entire color gradient ... (11 October 2026)
