### Workflows `heightfields_cop/workflows` (11 October 2026)

- Summary: Fundamental concepts for Copernicus terrains. (11 October 2026)
- COP nodes cited: blend, bubblenoise, cellpattern, curlnoise, fractalnoise, fractalnoise3d, fractalsample, heightfield_erosion, heightfield_slump, heightfield_strata, heightfield_terrace, monotoheightfield, phasornoise (11 October 2026)
- The following chapters illustrate fundamental workflows in conjunction with heightfields in Copernicus. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Noise (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus provides a great variety of noise types, for example the Bubble Noise COP, Phasor Noise COP, or Curl Noise COP nodes. (11 October 2026)
- Noise also plays an important role in the creation of the terrain's topography. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Blending (11 October 2026)
- You can use the Blend COP to combine heightfields with different topography, for example with Simplex and Perlin noise. (11 October 2026)
- The blend node has fg and bg inputs. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Erosion (11 October 2026)
- Erosion removes material from the surface, transports it, and deposits it in lower areas. (11 October 2026)
- The parameters of the Hydro section simulate the influence of water on the creation of trenches. (11 October 2026)
- When you connect an erosion node, Houdini will immediately start to remove material from the terrain. (11 October 2026)
- * Add a HeightField Erode COP and connect its height input with the height output of the stratify node. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Slump (11 October 2026)
- When loose stones and rocks become unstable, they slide down for a rather short distance and form out deposits in lower areas. (11 October 2026)
- You can find more information and an example how to use flow maps on the slump page. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Terraces (11 October 2026)
- Terraces are an important element with terrains in general. (11 October 2026)
- To get rid of the artificial and blocky look, you often apply one or more erosion passes. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Strata (11 October 2026)
- Strata indicate different layers of rock, for example layers of sediment from ancient oceans. (11 October 2026)
- On the Pattern section, you can find a Tilt parameter to adjust the inclination of the layers. (11 October 2026)
- * Lay down a HeightField Strata COP and connect its height input with the blend output of the Blend COP. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Masks (11 October 2026)
- Masking is one of the most important concepts in conjunction with realistic terrains. (11 October 2026)
- * Add a noise node, for example the Fractal Noise COP and adjust it. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Boulders (11 October 2026)
- Boulders and rocks that cover valleys and lower parts are the icing on the cake. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Colors and textures (11 October 2026)
- Houdini's Copernicus heightfields ship with a long list of predefined color ramps for various terrain types like rock, snow, tundras, or deserts. (11 October 2026)
- For more information, please read the colors page. (11 October 2026)
- Section: Conversion (11 October 2026)
- You can export terrain geometry to SOPs for further processing, or bring it to Solaris for rendering. (11 October 2026)
- There are various methods for converting Copernicus heightfields. (11 October 2026)
